lesson.body:
You already know from composition that an object can hold other objects as attributes. The has-a relationship is just the name for that pattern, and it is worth naming explicitly because it is one of the two big ways classes relate to each other in object-oriented programming. The other way, which you will meet in the next few sections, is the is-a relationship built through inheritance. Learning to tell the two apart early will save you from a lot of confused, over-engineered class hierarchies later.

A has-a relationship means exactly what it sounds like: an instance of one class stores an instance of another class as one of its attributes. A Car has an Engine. A Library has a collection of Book objects. A Playlist has a list of Song objects. In each case, the outer object does not become the inner object, it simply owns one, calls its methods, and reads its attributes when needed. This is composition in action, and it tends to produce flexible designs, because the two classes stay independent. You can change how Engine works internally, and Car does not care, as long as Engine still offers the methods Car expects.

Here is a small example. Notice that Car never claims to be an Engine, it just keeps one around and uses it.

```python
class Engine:
    def __init__(self, horsepower):
        self.horsepower = horsepower

    def start(self):
        return "Engine roars to life."

class Car:
    def __init__(self, horsepower):
        self.engine = Engine(horsepower)  # Car HAS-A Engine

    def start(self):
        return self.engine.start()

my_car = Car(300)
print(my_car.start())
print(my_car.engine.horsepower)
```

Running this prints exactly two lines: `Engine roars to life.` and then `300`. The `Car.start` method does not reimplement anything about starting an engine, it just forwards the call to the Engine object it holds in `self.engine`. This is the key benefit of has-a relationships: behavior is reused by delegation, by asking another object to do the work, rather than by copying code or forcing an artificial family relationship between classes.

Keep this pattern in your back pocket as you move into inheritance next. A good rule of thumb that professional developers rely on is "favor composition over inheritance": if you catch yourself unsure whether two classes should be related by has-a or is-a, has-a is usually the safer, more flexible default.
